  "summary": "Today's health-tech developments reflect three major themes shaping the sector. First, significant capital is flowing into specialized care and infrastructure: Angle Health raised $600 million for employer benefits, Electra Therapeutics secured $350 million via IPO for precision immunology drugs, and multiple virtual care platforms including Ayble Health and senior living AI company Inspiren closed substantial funding rounds. Second, regulatory and operational challenges persist despite recent innovations—the healthcare system still lacks redundancy in critical clearinghouse infrastructure two years after Change Healthcare's cyberattack, Medicare Advantage denial rates are climbing prompting health systems to steer patients to traditional Medicare, and hospital operating margins fell to 1.4% in July amid payer pressures and surgical slowdowns. Third, drug approvals and M&A activity remain robust, with FDA approvals for Ultragenyx's gene therapy for Sanfilippo syndrome and Scholar Rock's first-in-class neuromuscular disease treatment, while Novartis acquired blood-brain barrier technology from Sironax for $125 million and GSK committed $750 million for a T cell engager from Chimagen. Finally, policymakers are reshaping healthcare: RFK Jr. reorganized the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force leadership, and CMS is expanding its ACCESS outcomes-based payment model to heart failure, COPD, substance use disorders, and tobacco cessation beginning in spring 2027.",
